From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] modem_v1: arm the CP-to-AP interrupts for wake-up only while CP runs

The SS333 modem driver registers three interrupts on always-on (GPA)
pins, and mif_request_irq() arms every one of them as a wake-up source
the moment it is requested:

  ss333_active      PHONE_ACTIVE  gpa2-3  rising edge, left enabled
  lli_cp2ap_wakeup  CP2AP_WAKEUP  gpa3-1  level, disabled until CP_START
  lli_cp2ap_status  CP2AP_STATUS  gpa2-5  level, disabled until CP_START

The pins have no pull (exynos5433-modem-ss333.dtsi) and the GPA block
has no separate sleep configuration, so with CP powered off (CP_ON and
CP_RST low, which is how the driver leaves them at probe) the three
inputs float, awake and in suspend alike. On a tablet that never boots
its modem this has two effects.

1. Every rising edge on PHONE_ACTIVE can take the SoC out of suspend:
   arming the interrupt clears the pin's bit in EINT_WAKEUP_MASK. The
   handler returns at once while CP is not ONLINE, so each such wake-up
   is pure overhead.

2. The two LLI interrupts are disabled with disable_irq_nosync(), which
   is lazy here because the wake-up EINT chip has no irq_disable
   callback: the line stays unmasked. The first time a floating line
   reads high, handle_level_irq() masks it and sets IRQS_PENDING, and
   nothing clears that flag again before enable_irq().
   check_wakeup_irqs() in this tree returns -EBUSY for any wake-up
   interrupt with IRQS_PENDING, whether it is disabled or not ("Wakeup
   IRQ %d %s pending, suspend aborted"), so from then on no suspend
   succeeds until the next reboot.

Observed on an SM-T815 (hardware revision 6) running without cbd, 51
minutes after boot, in /proc/interrupts: ss333_active 13,
lli_cp2ap_wakeup 1, lli_cp2ap_status 1. A count that stops at 1 is what
a disabled level interrupt shows after its only possible delivery. No
suspend had been attempted in that session (USB connected), so neither
effect has been seen in a suspend log yet.

Arm the interrupts only while CP runs:

- mif_request_irq_nowake() requests an interrupt without arming it.
  mif_request_irq() keeps its old behaviour.
- mif_enable_irq_wake() and mif_disable_irq_wake() arm and disarm. They
  remember the state in struct modem_irq and do nothing when it is
  already as asked, so the enable_irq_wake()/disable_irq_wake() pairs
  cannot become unbalanced whatever order the modem control paths run
  in.
- An interrupt is armed right after it is enabled and disarmed right
  before it is disabled. "Armed" therefore implies "enabled", and the
  state check_wakeup_irqs() trips over - armed, disabled and pending -
  cannot be reached any more, neither before the first boot of CP nor
  after CP has been switched off again.

ss333_active, by path:

  ss333_init_modemctl_device  requested, enabled, not armed
                              (was: enabled and armed)
  ss333_on                    disarm (nothing to do before the first
                              boot), disable
  ss333_boot_off              enable, arm; on the init timeout it
                              returns before both, leaving the
                              interrupt disabled and not armed
  ss333_boot_done             no change
  ss333_off                   disarm, disable; also reached through
                              modem_shutdown() and ss333_reset()
  ss333_force_crash_exit      does not touch the interrupt: it stays
                              enabled and armed while CP is still
                              powered, as before
  ss333_dump_reset            disarm, disable
  cp_active_handler           does not touch it

lli_cp2ap_wakeup and lli_cp2ap_status, by path:

  init_link_device_pm         requested, disabled, not armed
                              (was: disabled and armed)
  start_link_pm               enable, arm, for all three start events;
                              a second call returns early ("already
                              ACTIVE")
  stop_link_pm                disarm, disable; returns early when PM is
                              not active. Reached from lli_link_off(),
                              i.e. from ss333_on, ss333_off,
                              ss333_force_crash_exit and
                              ss333_dump_reset, and from
                              pm_cp_fail_cb()

With CP booted by cbd the three interrupts are armed exactly while they
are enabled, which is when a wake-up from them can be acted upon. The
windows in which they used to be armed but disabled (CP booting, crash
dump, CP off) are covered by ss333_wake_lock or have CP powered down.
The enable and disable calls themselves, and with them the replay of an
edge that arrives while ss333_active is disabled, are unchanged.

ss333_active is deliberately not disabled before the first boot. It is
edge triggered, and an edge latched while it is disabled is replayed
when ss333_boot_off() enables it; cp_active_handler() takes a rising
edge with CP ONLINE and PHONE_ACTIVE high for a CP crash. Keeping the
interrupt disabled from probe would let the floating line queue such an
edge for the first boot. Left enabled, as it always was, a stray edge is
consumed by the handler, which ignores it while CP is not ONLINE; not
being armed, it cannot wake the system.

A disabled LLI interrupt can still latch IRQS_PENDING on a floating
line. That is harmless now: check_wakeup_irqs() only looks at armed
interrupts, and start_link_pm() enables the interrupt, which drops the
flag for a level interrupt, before it arms it.

Other users: modem_ctrl_ss300.c and the non-FSM half of
link_device_lli.c still call mif_request_irq() and are not changed
(neither is built with lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig). link_device_pm.c
is shared by every modem that uses
CONFIG_LINK_POWER_MANAGEMENT_WITH_FSM; its two interrupts follow the new
rule on those boards too.
---
 drivers/misc/modem_v1/link_device_pm.c   |  17 ++++-
 drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_ctrl_ss333.c |  11 ++-
 drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.c      | 105 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.h      |   4 ++
 include/linux/platform_data/modem_v1.h   |   1 +
 5 files changed, 135 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/link_device_pm.c b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/link_device_pm.c
index ac63869..7327637 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/link_device_pm.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/link_device_pm.c
@@ -822,14 +822,17 @@ static void start_link_pm(struct modem_link_pm *pm, enum pm_event event)
 
 	/*
 	Enable every CP-to-AP IRQ and set it as a wake-up source
+	(enable first: that drops what a disabled level IRQ has left pending)
 	*/
 	cp2ap_wakeup = gpio_get_value(pm->gpio_cp2ap_wakeup);
 	change_irq_level(pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq.num, cp2ap_wakeup);
 	mif_enable_irq(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq);
+	mif_enable_irq_wake(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq);
 
 	cp2ap_status = gpio_get_value(pm->gpio_cp2ap_status);
 	change_irq_level(pm->cp2ap_status_irq.num, cp2ap_status);
 	mif_enable_irq(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq);
+	mif_enable_irq_wake(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq);
 
 	set_pm_fsm(pm, PM_STATE_UNMOUNTED, state, event);
 
@@ -856,10 +859,16 @@ static void stop_link_pm(struct modem_link_pm *pm)
 
 	pm->active = false;
 
+	/*
+	A disabled IRQ must not stay a wake-up source: the lines float while CP
+	is off, and a wake-up IRQ left pending aborts every suspend
+	*/
+	mif_disable_irq_wake(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq);
 	mif_disable_irq(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq);
 	mif_err("%s: PM %s_irq#%d handler disabled\n", pm->link_name,
 		pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq.name, pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq.num);
 
+	mif_disable_irq_wake(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq);
 	mif_disable_irq(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq);
 	mif_err("%s: PM %s_irq#%d handler disabled\n", pm->link_name,
 		pm->cp2ap_status_irq.name, pm->cp2ap_status_irq.num);
@@ -933,11 +942,14 @@ int init_link_device_pm(struct link_device *ld,
 
 	/*
 	Register cp2ap_wakeup IRQ handler
+	(not as a wake-up source yet: start_link_pm() arms both CP-to-AP IRQs
+	and stop_link_pm() disarms them)
 	*/
 	cp2ap_wakeup = gpio_get_value(pm->gpio_cp2ap_wakeup);
 	change_irq_level(pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq.num, cp2ap_wakeup);
 
-	err = mif_request_irq(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq, cp2ap_wakeup_handler, pm);
+	err = mif_request_irq_nowake(&pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq,
+				     cp2ap_wakeup_handler, pm);
 	if (err) {
 		mif_err("%s: ERR! request_irq(%s#%d) fail (%d)\n",
 			pm->link_name, pm->cp2ap_wakeup_irq.name,
@@ -955,7 +967,8 @@ int init_link_device_pm(struct link_device *ld,
 	cp2ap_status = gpio_get_value(pm->gpio_cp2ap_status);
 	change_irq_level(pm->cp2ap_status_irq.num, cp2ap_status);
 
-	err = mif_request_irq(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq, cp2ap_status_handler, pm);
+	err = mif_request_irq_nowake(&pm->cp2ap_status_irq,
+				     cp2ap_status_handler, pm);
 	if (err) {
 		mif_err("%s: ERR! request_irq(%s#%d) fail (%d)\n",
 			pm->link_name, pm->cp2ap_status_irq.name,
diff --git a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_ctrl_ss333.c b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_ctrl_ss333.c
index 2351b85..670fd9c 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_ctrl_ss333.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_ctrl_ss333.c
@@ -162,6 +162,7 @@ static int ss333_on(struct modem_ctl *mc)
 	struct link_device *ld = get_current_link(iod);
 	unsigned long flags;
 
+	mif_disable_irq_wake(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 	mif_disable_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 
 	mif_info("%s: %s: +++\n", mc->name, FUNC);
@@ -215,6 +216,7 @@ static int ss333_off(struct modem_ctl *mc)
 	unsigned long flags;
 	int i;
 
+	mif_disable_irq_wake(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 	mif_disable_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 
 	mif_info("%s: %s: +++\n", mc->name, FUNC);
@@ -342,6 +344,7 @@ static int ss333_dump_reset(struct modem_ctl *mc)
 	unsigned int gpio_cp_reset = mc->gpio_cp_reset;
 	unsigned long flags;
 
+	mif_disable_irq_wake(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 	mif_disable_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 
 	mif_info("%s: %s: +++\n", mc->name, FUNC);
@@ -416,6 +419,7 @@ static int ss333_boot_off(struct modem_ctl *mc)
 	}
 
 	mif_enable_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active);
+	mif_enable_irq_wake(&mc->irq_cp_active);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&mc->lock, flags);
 	mc->bootd->modem_state_changed(mc->bootd, STATE_ONLINE);
@@ -572,7 +576,12 @@ int ss333_init_modemctl_device(struct modem_ctl *mc, struct modem_data *pdata)
 	snprintf(name, MAX_NAME_LEN, "%s_active", mc->name);
 	mif_init_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active, irq, name, flag);
 
-	ret = mif_request_irq(&mc->irq_cp_active, cp_active_handler, mc);
+	/*
+	PHONE_ACTIVE is not driven while CP is off and the pin has no pull, so
+	the IRQ must not be a wake-up source before CP has been booted. It is
+	armed in ss333_boot_off() and disarmed again wherever it is disabled.
+	*/
+	ret = mif_request_irq_nowake(&mc->irq_cp_active, cp_active_handler, mc);
 	if (ret) {
 		mif_err("%s: ERR! request_irq(%s#%d) fail (%d)\n",
 			mc->name, mc->irq_cp_active.name,
diff --git a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.c b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.c
index 5d6a8b6..679fbeb 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.c
@@ -1044,10 +1044,19 @@ void mif_init_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, unsigned int num, const char *name,
 	irq->num = num;
 	strncpy(irq->name, name, (MAX_NAME_LEN - 1));
 	irq->flags = flags;
+	irq->wake = false;
 	mif_info("%s: name:%s num:%d flags:0x%08lX\n",
 		FUNC, name, num, flags);
 }
 
+/**
+@brief		request an IRQ and arm it as a wake-up source at once
+
+@remark		The IRQ stays armed for as long as nobody calls
+		mif_disable_irq_wake(), also while it is disabled. Use
+		mif_request_irq_nowake() for an IRQ whose line is not driven
+		while the modem is off.
+*/
 int mif_request_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr, void *data)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -1065,6 +1074,8 @@ int mif_request_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr, void *data)
 	if (ret) {
 		mif_err("%s: %s: ERR! enable_irq_wake fail (%d)\n",
 			FUNC, irq->name, ret);
+	} else {
+		irq->wake = true;
 	}
 
 	mif_info("%s: %s(#%d) handler registered (flags:0x%08lX)\n",
@@ -1073,6 +1084,33 @@ int mif_request_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr, void *data)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/**
+@brief		request an IRQ without arming it as a wake-up source
+
+The IRQ is left enabled, exactly as mif_request_irq() leaves it, but it can
+neither wake the system up nor make check_wakeup_irqs() abort a suspend until
+mif_enable_irq_wake() is called.
+*/
+int mif_request_irq_nowake(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr,
+			   void *data)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = request_irq(irq->num, isr, irq->flags, irq->name, data);
+	if (ret) {
+		mif_err("%s: %s: ERR! request_irq fail (%d)\n",
+			FUNC, irq->name, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	irq->active = true;
+
+	mif_info("%s: %s(#%d) handler registered (flags:0x%08lX)\n",
+		FUNC, irq->name, irq->num, irq->flags);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 void mif_enable_irq(struct modem_irq *irq)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
@@ -1119,6 +1157,73 @@ exit:
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&irq->lock, flags);
 }
 
+/**
+@brief		arm an IRQ as a wake-up source
+
+Does nothing if the IRQ is armed already, so that the callers need not keep
+the enable_irq_wake()/disable_irq_wake() calls balanced themselves.
+
+@remark		Arm an IRQ only while it is enabled. This kernel aborts every
+		suspend for as long as a wake-up IRQ has IRQS_PENDING set, and
+		a disabled IRQ keeps that flag until it is enabled again.
+*/
+void mif_enable_irq_wake(struct modem_irq *irq)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&irq->lock, flags);
+
+	if (irq->wake)
+		goto exit;
+
+	ret = enable_irq_wake(irq->num);
+	if (ret) {
+		mif_err("%s: %s(#%d) ERR! enable_irq_wake fail (%d) <%pf>\n",
+			FUNC, irq->name, irq->num, ret, CALLER);
+		goto exit;
+	}
+
+	irq->wake = true;
+
+	mif_info("%s: %s(#%d) is armed for wake-up <%pf>\n",
+		FUNC, irq->name, irq->num, CALLER);
+
+exit:
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&irq->lock, flags);
+}
+
+/**
+@brief		stop an IRQ from being a wake-up source
+
+Does nothing if the IRQ is not armed.
+*/
+void mif_disable_irq_wake(struct modem_irq *irq)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&irq->lock, flags);
+
+	if (!irq->wake)
+		goto exit;
+
+	ret = disable_irq_wake(irq->num);
+	if (ret) {
+		mif_err("%s: %s(#%d) ERR! disable_irq_wake fail (%d) <%pf>\n",
+			FUNC, irq->name, irq->num, ret, CALLER);
+		goto exit;
+	}
+
+	irq->wake = false;
+
+	mif_info("%s: %s(#%d) is disarmed for wake-up <%pf>\n",
+		FUNC, irq->name, irq->num, CALLER);
+
+exit:
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&irq->lock, flags);
+}
+
 struct file *mif_open_file(const char *path)
 {
 	struct file *fp;
diff --git a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.h b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.h
index da7c661..eb2d88f 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.h
+++ b/drivers/misc/modem_v1/modem_utils.h
@@ -520,8 +520,12 @@ bool is_syn_packet(const u8 *ip_pkt);
 void mif_init_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, unsigned int num, const char *name,
 		  unsigned long flags);
 int mif_request_irq(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr, void *data);
+int mif_request_irq_nowake(struct modem_irq *irq, irq_handler_t isr,
+			   void *data);
 void mif_enable_irq(struct modem_irq *irq);
 void mif_disable_irq(struct modem_irq *irq);
+void mif_enable_irq_wake(struct modem_irq *irq);
+void mif_disable_irq_wake(struct modem_irq *irq);
 
 struct file *mif_open_file(const char *path);
 void mif_save_file(struct file *fp, const char *buff, size_t size);
diff --git a/include/linux/platform_data/modem_v1.h b/include/linux/platform_data/modem_v1.h
index 60136fb..87e2e32 100644
--- a/include/linux/platform_data/modem_v1.h
+++ b/include/linux/platform_data/modem_v1.h
@@ -471,6 +471,7 @@ struct modem_irq {
 	char name[MAX_NAME_LEN];
 	unsigned long flags;
 	bool active;
+	bool wake;	/* armed as a wake-up source (enable_irq_wake) */
 };
 
 #define MODEM_BOOT_DEV_SPI "spi_boot_link"
